Proposals have to be submitted in accordance with the BAF Proposal Form. Eligibility criteria include:
Small-scale innovation grants:
• Alignment with the Blue Abadi Strategic Plan;
• Leadership roles for indigenous communities and/or women and children;
• Innovation and scalability of the proposed project.
Medium-scale grants:
• Alignment with Blue Abadi’s Strategic Plan and with the management plan for the conservation
area in the Bird's Head Seascape;
• Performance in previous years and other evidence that the applicant possesses the technical
capacity required to successfully implement project activities;
• Financial transparency and quality of reporting;
• compliance with Blue Abadi’s  Gender and Indigenous Peoples Mainstreaming Plan; and
• Leadership roles for indigenous communities and/or women and children (preferred, but not required)
Additional requirements for primary grants:
• Demonstrated commitment to balancing costs and increasing local revenue sources.

Prospective recipients submit an application letter to the Director General of BPDLH. Undated documents from the South Kalimantan province indicate that for direct disbursement of loan funds, prospective FDB recipients should be members of a Community Forest Farmers’ Group (KTHR) and submit KTHR documents. These documents can include including:
1. KTHR Information
2. Legal documents regarding the establishment of the KTHR, consisting of the minutes of the KTHR’s formation and the KTHR’s Articles of Association or other regulatory documents of the KTHR;
3. Documents regarding land access for the KTHR utilizing forest areas (e.g., social forestry permits or cooperation agreements with area managers);
4. A letter of recommendation from a facilitator

This should be supplemented with documents for each KTHR member, consisting of:
1. A revolving fund facility application form signed by each applicant;
2. Copies of the applicant’s and spouse’s ID cards
3. A photocopy of the applicant’s family registration card
4. Copies of land ownership documents (for on-farm businesses outside forest areas) or business registration documents/business permits/statements regarding raw material sources (for off-farm businesses);
5. Data on forestry business assets, such as a tally sheet corresponding to the loan amount.

Collateral for 100% of the loan amount has to be provided, through movable assets, immovable assets, current receivables, or cash collateral.

Applications are vetted through desk and on-field analysis.

Eligibility criteria are not identified, but a process is laid out for participation in NEK mechanisms. For example,  to obtain GHG emission offsets, project proponents must:
- submit the DRAM (project climate change mitigation action plan) or DPP (project planning document) to the Relevant Minister for registration;
- validate the DRAM or DPP through an Independent Validation Body;
- verify the achievements of Climate Change Mitigation Actions through an Independent Verification Body; and
- report the results of the verification of the achievements of Climate Change Mitigation Actions to the Relevant Minister.

